  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAlthough guidance has not handled it well, models remain insistent \nin the low levels starting to dry this afternoon, which should \nlead to the stubborn deck of lower clouds eventually lifting and \nthinning out. Until then, drizzle is possible at times for the \nnext couple of hours, mainly for SW Maine where cloud bases are \ncurrently lower. Prospects of seeing the sun this afternoon are \nfairly low already and are further dwindling as we're already \nseeing high clouds moving in from the west, which should \ncontinue to increase and thicken this evening and tonight as a\n500mb shortwave approaches. So highs for this afternoon have \nbeen knocked down a couple of degrees, but conversely these \nclouds should help limit radiational cooling tonight to keep \ntemps mostly in the lower 50s. I do have patchy fog for most \narea tonight into Thursday morning, and HREF probabilities are \nreally highlighting fog for areas along and east of the I-95 \ncorridor in western ME, but coverage is in question due to the \ncloud cover. \n\nHigh pressure remains in control to keep conditions dry for the most \npart on Thursday. Model soundings depict some drying behind the wave \nthat moves through tonight, so we should see more sun, at least\nthrough the first part of the day. Clouds then start increasing\nfrom west to east through the afternoon, more noticeably in NH \n(where a few sprinkles are possible), as moisture trends upward \nahead of a frontal boundary. Otherwise, 850mb temperatures of \n+10C to +12C support highs reaching the mid-upper 70s, which \nwill be roughly 10 degrees above normal for the first day of \nOctober. \n\nClouds further increase Thursday night with a light S/SW breeze, \nwhich will both help hinder fog development and keep overnight lows \nvery mild in the 50s and 60s. A few showers are possible in the \nmountains, but most will be dry.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nFri will be one more very warm day as we remain in the WAA ahead of \nthe next approaching front. All guidance is pointing towards 10 to \n15 degrees above normal across the forecast area. This will mean \nsome locations may make a run at 80 degrees. Very warm for sure, but \nlooking to be at least 5 degrees below records for this time of \nyear. The NBM may also be running slightly warm as we start to \ntransition out of the warm season and into the cold season.\n\nPrecip with the front will be showery in nature and forcing not \nstrong along the boundary. I suspect that some areas will miss out \non rainfall or not amount to much.\n\nThis first front will serve to knock temps back to normal for the \nweekend. With the clear and calm night looking to be Sat night, that \nshould be the coldest. I anticipate some frost into the foothills, \nbut those zones will no longer be active in the frost/freeze \nprogram. However a stronger shortwave is forecast to cross the \nregion late Sun into Mon. This is much lower confidence on rainfall. \nEnsemble members are less than 50/50 on measurable rainfall across \nthe forecast area, especially the farther south you go. There is \nhigher confidence that some cooler than normal air follows in its \nwake. Tue temps may not get out of the 50s under mostly sunny skies \nalong with breezy westerly winds. &&",
